rota lap 5387, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; the reading saw ten where the ledger booked one, which is the row's own lesson about looking rather than recalling
think My previous lap opened claim incense-commit-parent-hash-claim at a54781b24 and ended before building. The claim was 20 minutes old and pushed, so this lap is its build rather than a fresh claim.
think The runner refused a second cold pass: the prior pass's launch_head a54781b241 was no longer HEAD, so its verdict was already fixed. TERMed it by the pid the runner itself printed -- never a pattern -- and relaunched at the settled HEAD with --cadence-slice 1.
think Held still for the whole pass, writing every draft under .lap/, which the digest keeps outside both halves. tree_moved=no, 335 green, 18 red, 2 gated; all 18 stand on the card as the fleet's inherited set and none is mine. The slice turned shell_emit_ascii, last=never.
think Measured %801's class before designing rather than reasoning from the row: over 4,000 commit bodies, 96 sentences bind a hash token to the word parent, 55 assert the relation about themselves, 36 name the true first parent.
think The ten that name something else split three ways, and the split is what decided the instrument: 6 name a hash no object store on this pier holds, 1 names a commit on an abandoned branch, 3 name a live ancestor that is not the parent.
think One of the ten landed twelve hours AFTER the row was booked -- d61898646c at 13:01 today, naming 94df5c9c2b where the parent is 2effbdc356. A defect recurring after it is written down is a defect a guard owes.
think Checked resolvability across four checkouts rather than assuming: five of the six ghosts resolve on none of them and one resolves on exactly one peer, so resolvability is per-checkout and can never grade a peer's prose.
think Made the gate forward-only against a named anchor. Testimony can never be repaired, so a wall over the ten would red on every ship for a fault no lap may take -- which is a wall somebody turns off.
obs Mine, twice. My scan header claimed the at-least-one-letter clause kept ed25519 out of the reading; the pen proved it a token on the first run, seven characters and every one hexadecimal. The RELATION filter is what holds it, and the header now says so.
obs Mine again, and worse. My control's say read *=no while want emits no (want ...), so five failing legs stood under control_verdict=ok. Fixed the pattern and rebuilt every leg to reset to one spine, since five of them had been reading a neighbour leg's commit.
obs What no hook can reach: at commit-msg time git rev-parse HEAD is the prospective parent for a fresh commit and the commit being replaced for an amend, and nothing the hook reads tells them apart. That is %803's structural twin one rule over, and it is why the cure is a guard rather than a wall in tools/hooks/commit-msg.
obs The card stood 369 bytes under its bound, so the account was shelved whole and the new one written compact against the paper. Four trims to fit; 40949 of 40960.
